---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import CodeWindow from "../components/CodeWindow.astro";
import PNotes from "../components/PNotes.astro";
import consoleLog from "../img/console_log_worker.png";
import sofarSogood from "../img/sofar-sogood.jpg";
---

<p-slide layout="center">
  <h1>
    <span aria-hidden="true">⚙️</span> <span gradient>Web Workers</span>
    <span aria-hidden="true">⚙️</span>
  </h1>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <ul style="line-height: 1.8em">
    <li>
      🐣 <span gradient="sub">Born in 2009</span>
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">
      🧮 <span gradient="sub">Multi-threaded code execution</span>
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      🔒 <span gradient="sub">Fully isolated</span>
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="zoom">
      ✅ <span gradient="sub">Not framework related</span>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <PNotes>
    <p>
      Fully isolated: a web worker cannot access the DOM and main thread, but
      can only send and receive messages
    </p>
    <p>
      Not framework related: a web worker can be used in any JavaScript
      environment
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>
<p-slide cover>
  <h1>Let's see how it works</h1>
  <span layout="center" aria-hidden="true">
    👀
  </span>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <h4 layout="center" style="margin-block-end: 0.5em">
    Let's Code <span aria-hidden="true">👨‍💻</span>
  </h4>
  <CodeWindow is:raw file="worker.js" lang="js">
    self.onmessage = (event) => {
      console.log("Worker received:", event.data);
      // Do heavy stuff
      self.postMessage(`Hello from worker!`);
    };
  </CodeWindow>
  <p-fragment>
    <span
      sketch-circle
      style="--sketch-height: 1.8em; position: absolute; inset-block-end: 8em; inline-size: 4em; inset-inline-start: 6.5em; z-index: 1"
    ></span>
  </p-fragment>
  <p-fragment>
    <span
      sketch-circle
      style="--sketch-height: 1.8em; position: absolute; inset-block-end: 5.7em; inline-size: 5em; inset-inline-start: 3.5em; z-index: 1"
    ></span>
  </p-fragment>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <h4 layout="center" style="margin-block-end: 0.5em">
    Let's Code <span aria-hidden="true">👨‍💻</span>
  </h4>
  <CodeWindow is:raw file="main.js" lang="js" fragments="1, 3-6, 8-9">
    const worker = new Worker("worker.js");

    worker.onmessage = (event) => {
      console.log("Main thread received:", event.data);
      // Do some other stuff
    };

    // Starting point
    worker.postMessage('Hello from main thread');
  </CodeWindow>
  <PNotes>
    Web Worker in the browser is native—in the sense that: It is managed by the
    browser underlying engine (like V8 in Chrome, SpiderMonkey in Firefox, or
    JavaScriptCore in Safari). The worker runs in its own OS-level thread,
    separate from the main UI thread. This allows it to perform operations
    concurrently without blocking the main thread. So, when you create a new
    Worker(...), the browser spins up a separate thread using native system APIs
    under the hood. However, from the JavaScript developer’s perspective, Web
    Workers are not threads you control directly—you just post messages back and
    forth using postMessage() and onmessage.
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <h4 layout="center" style="margin-block-end: 0.5em">
    ◝(ᵔᗜᵔ)◜
  </h4>
  <Image
    src={consoleLog}
    alt="Browser console showing the messages exchanged by the main thread and the worker"
    style="--size: 7.5em"
  />
</p-slide>
<p-slide>
  <Image src={sofarSogood} alt="“So far so good” meme" style="--size: 8em" />
</p-slide>
